qa/round7-reviews.md:4:- [high] Generated levels have almost no real decisions, and difficulty goes down over time. I traced the solver on each winning line. L12: 10 taps and 30 alternative options. Only 1 option loses later (s2: tapping the green pair early). The other 3 losing options are instant bench overflows, which is plain arithmetic. L30: 13 taps. The only planning mistake is 'mint pair early', offered 3 turns in a row (s3-s5), so it is one decision. L60 (8x11, 18 cats): 15 taps, dead ratio 0.044, and 1 lose-later option in about 45. It is easier than L12 (0.133) and L30 (0.152). Over 90% of taps win whatever you pick, so players do not need to think.
qa/round7-reviews.md:6:- [high] Every level ends the same way. In all 3 traces the bench climbs to peak 4, a [3,1] double chain fires, and then 2-5 taps follow with free=1 (L12 s8-9, L30 s9-12, L60 s10-14). check-core shows peak 4 and bench 5 in every range from L5 to L500. So the chain, the one clip moment, always happens on a nearly empty board (28f/28g: 'Chain x3!' over an empty grid), and the last third of each level is forced cleanup.
qa/round7-reviews.md:8:- [high] Mechanics are sparse in mid-game. L30 and L60 have zero dog, yarn, mystery or frozen pieces. check-core shows L12-39 with 19 pieces over 28 levels, and L55-69 with 21 over 15. Frozen is taught at L55, and the very next sample, L60, has none. The dog in the teaching level (24c/24e) only wakes when the board is nearly empty, so it never changed the solution.
qa/round7-reviews.md:18:- [medium] The share clip has little going for it. clip score is 2 for the normal win and 4 for the chain win. The chain happens on an empty board (28f/28g), and the win card preview is a tiny static board (28d/28h). Nothing in it would make someone stop scrolling.
