**Put #1–4 in the owner’s next playtest.** Round 7 found two versions of the same problem. The next tap is hard to read, and once you can read it, the puzzle keeps asking the same question. The generator holds back big cats and pairs, then aims for one spare seat. The café finishes at L60. [Round 7 reviews](<C:/Users/Admin/Documents/Msjelly/studio/long-cat-out/qa/round7-reviews.md>) · [Core metrics](<C:/Users/Admin/Documents/Msjelly/studio/long-cat-out/qa/core-metrics.txt>)

1. **Show where the cat will land. M.** Put three fill pips on the active carrier, number the queue `1–3`, and label the bench `4/5`. On press, draw the exit ray and mark the exact cushion or carrier for each cat. Label split arrivals `1 Mom` and `2 Kitten`. Flash `No seat` at the bench. Release to send, drag away to cancel. Use a read-only rules preview and route by carrier ID. **Why:** Players can spend their attention on the choice. **Verify:** At least 8 of 10 new phone players predict both destinations on a split-family move within three seconds.

2. **Generate five different decisions. L.** Author two boards per pattern before generalizing. All examples use five cushions. The generator should vary the geometry around these decisions.

   | Pattern | Setup, temptation, consequence |
   |---|---|
   | **Open the right lane** | Orange needs one. The bench is 4/5. A long Blue is free and Blue is next. A short Mint is the sole blocker of the last Orange. Blue takes the only cushion Mint needs. |
   | **Save two cushions** | The bench is 3/5. A Blue family blocks the last Orange. A free Pink belongs to the next carrier. Pink looks like a smart early park, but leaves the family one cushion short. |
   | **Send the singleton first** | Orange needs one. Two Blue and two later cats are parked. Blue is next. The free Orange family looks ideal, but Mom departs and Kitten parks after the Blues board. That extra cushion prevents the three off-color exits needed to reach the final Blue. Send the free Orange singleton first. |
   | **Stage the double departure** | Orange needs one. Two Blues are parked. The last Blue and Orange are both free. Orange first looks efficient and still wins. Park Blue first to send both carriers on one tap and earn the Show target. |
   | **Find the shared blocker** | Orange needs two. The bench is 3/5. Two obvious Blue blockers each free one Orange. One tucked-away Mint frees both. Blue is next, so parking both Blues looks efficient. Both routes win, but Mint meets a four-seat Show target. |

   **Why:** The clue and the consequence change from board to board. **Verify:** Keep the family patterns below one-third of any 20-level block. On medium boards, require two distinct, proven losing choices that fit on the bench when tapped. Count a repeated bait cat once. Sample three winning routes. Enforce both ends of the difficulty band and use a vetted fallback. Solver timeouts earn zero credit. [Generator](<C:/Users/Admin/Documents/Msjelly/studio/long-cat-out/src/core/generate.js:793>) · [Decision search](<C:/Users/Admin/Documents/Msjelly/studio/long-cat-out/src/core/search.js:356>)

3. **Move the payoff into the puzzle. M.** In each ten-level block, give four boards a double departure at 40–70% of taps with at least five cats still visible. Give three a single-carrier finish and three a decision in the last three taps. Drop the universal one-spare-seat target. **Why:** A chain can change what you do next, and the endings stop blending together. **Verify:** Replay all 600 levels. Reject boards with more than two trailing taps that have only one legal move.

4. **Teach the consequence, then give one take-back. S.** Hand-author L5–7 for opening a lane, reserving cushions, and staging a chain. On first overflow, keep the board bright. Trace the offending cat to its missing cushion. Offer **Take Back** and **Retry** side by side. Make the first take-back free. **Why:** A mistake gives the player something to try immediately. **Verify:** At least 8 of 10 testers can point to the move they’d change. Track retry and quit after first overflow.

5. **Make the chain the share card. M.** Briefly hold on the crowded bench, then stagger the hops and give each departure its own beat. Keep the chain count beside the belt. Loop 4–5 seconds at full win-card width, from one tap before the trigger through the second departure. Export a readable portrait clip with a **Play this board** link to the same versioned puzzle. **Why:** The clip shows a setup and payoff. The link gives the recipient something to beat. **Verify:** Share from iOS and Android. Track clip shares per eligible win, then recipient starts and clears.

6. **Make Cat Show about mastery. M.** Ship two fixed boards per week from L10. Free retries, no boosters. Each has one published goal, such as a four-seat peak or two departures on one tap. Give a clear medal and a gold medal. Share the exact board and target. **Why:** Players come back for a new puzzle and have a reason to replay it. **Verify:** Track second attempts after a clear, gold conversion, and return for the following week’s boards.

7. **Add one carrier swap at L24. M.** Once per board, swap queue slots 2 and 3. The active carrier stays put. Example with four cats parked and a Pink family blocking the last Blue: bringing Mint forward boards two parked Mints, freeing the cushions the family needs. **Why:** Players can change the queue to fit their plan. **Verify:** Author one board where swapping wins and one where saving it wins. Check swap, undo, preview, and solver agreement.

8. **Give the café a 12-week collection. M.** Run three four-week seasons using Show boards. Each unique clear earns one stamp and gold earns one more. Six stamps invite a permanent visiting cat. Give each visitor a portrait and one café interaction. Archive past boards with their stamps intact. Preview the next visitor before Rooftop ends. **Why:** There’s a visible goal after L60, and missed weeks stay playable. **Verify:** Track post-L60 return, second-season participation, and D30/D60 by cohort.

9. **Keep Yarn only where it changes a choice. S.** Teach it with two possible exit lanes and one knock that opens the cat needed for the active carrier. Reuse it in two of the next four boards. **Why:** A visible knock can be planned and feels good to trigger. **Verify:** Require the knock before halfway and prove that removing Yarn changes which tap keeps a win available.

10. **Sell optional help after players have a reason to stay. S.** Give the first cohort unlimited retries through L15. Keep rewarded continues behind **More options**. Test post-win interstitials separately from lives, starting at L16 with a five-minute cap between ads. **Why:** Preserve the retry loop while measuring revenue. **Verify:** Compare D1/D7 and revenue per new player. Keep a treatment only if revenue rises and D7 falls by less than one percentage point.

**Cut from the next build:** Sleeping Dog, Frozen, and hidden Mystery colors. The 44-ribbon grind, login calendar, booster-use chores, and repeatable Rooftop supply party. The fail-screen Starter Bundle, overlapping life and bench toasts, and combo text over the cats.
